---
// src/pages/projects/example-project.astro
import Layout from "../../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout title="Stock Scanner — Nicholas Grzelak">
  <article class="project-page">
    <header class="project-header">
      <p class="label">PB-005 · Personal Build</p>
      <h1>Stock Scanner</h1>
      <p class="project-lede">
        Unified app to track and manage equities in multiple currecies.
      </p>
      <p class="label project-facts">
        2022 · Python · UI/UX
      </p>
    </header>

    <!--
      Images: drop files into the /public folder of your project, e.g.
      public/projects/plotter/render-01.jpg, then reference them from the
      site root like below. Delete this placeholder block once you have
      real images.
    -->
    <figure class="project-figure">
      <!-- <div class="img-placeholder" aria-hidden="true">
        RENDER — ISO VIEW (replace with your image)
      </div> -->
        <img src="/projects/stockscan/StockScannerMain.jpg" alt="Stock Scanner Landing Page" />
      <!-- <img src="/projects/plotter/render-01.jpg" alt="Isometric CAD render of the plotter frame" /> -->
      <figcaption class="label">FIG. 1 — Stock Scanner Landing Page</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h2>Overview</h2>
    <p>
      This side project was designed becuase some web brokers do not unify holdings together and keep everything seperated by account (CAD vs USD)
    </p>

    <h2>Design</h2>
    <p>
      After learning about Dash Plotly during one of my internships I wanted to explore its features more and design a web app that I could use for stock tracking.
      This interactive web app was completely written in Python with packages such as: yfinance for data scrapping, Dash for UI, Plotly for graphing.
    </p>

    <figure class="project-figure">
      <!-- <div class="img-placeholder" aria-hidden="true">
        RENDER — ISO VIEW (replace with your image)
      </div> -->
        <img src="/projects/stockscan/stockscannergraph.jpg" alt="CRM login page" />
      <!-- <img src="/projects/plotter/render-01.jpg" alt="Isometric CAD render of the plotter frame" /> -->
      <figcaption class="label">FIG. 2 — Graphing feature of Stock Scanner</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h2>Results</h2>
    <p>
      While ambitions for the app were high including stock analysis and net portfolio gains many of those features had minimal implementation or never implemented at all.
      A variety of problems were present: yfinance would update frequently and break scrapping features, slow load times when more than 5 stocks were added (lots of data was downloaded)
      and limited time during the school year. If I were to make this again I would try another package instead of yfinance to prevent constant breaking. Although some data is cached and 
      data acquisitons were simplified constantly downloading all stock data each time the page was refreshed slowed down the interface. I would log data like the industry once
       and save historical prices so that only new data needed to be acquired. This project was not for waste though as it taught me dataframe management and honed my 
       skills in python. Both of those have helped me to this day.
    </p>

    <p class="back-link"><a href="/">← Back to index</a></p>
  </article>
</Layout>

<style>
  .project-page {
    padding: 4rem 0 1rem;
  }

  .project-header {
    margin-bottom: 3rem;
  }

  .project-header h1 {
    font-size: clamp(1.7rem, 4vw, 2.4rem);
    margin-top: 1rem;
  }

  .project-lede {
    margin-top: 1.25rem;
    max-width: 52ch;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
  }

  .project-facts {
    margin-top: 1.25rem;
  }

  .project-page h2 {
    font-size: 1.25rem;
    font-weight: 400;
    margin: 2.5rem 0 0.75rem;
  }

  .project-figure {
    margin: 2.5rem 0;
  }

  .project-figure figcaption {
    margin-top: 0.6rem;
  }

  .img-placeholder {
    display: grid;
    place-items: center;
    aspect-ratio: 16 / 9;
    border: 1px solid var(--rule-strong);
    font-family: var(--mono);
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    text-align: center;
    padding: 1rem;
  }

  .back-link {
    margin-top: 3.5rem;
  }
</style>